From d9fca3752b4cd2e0b31ff8e7627b626bcc23ebdc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ActiveAE: send PCM in 16 bits to a display that takes fewer
 than 24

The HDA HDMI codec of Linux offers a 32-bit format (S32_LE) wherever an
LPCM short audio descriptor of the display declares 20 or 24 bits, and
sends the display as many bits of it as the descriptors declare at most
(snd_hdmi_eld_update_pcm_info(), snd_hdac_stream_format_bits()): 20 bits
for a display that declares 16 and 20. Kodi asks the sink for float,
which HDA does not take, and then takes the widest format that the
output offers, S32. Kodi's ELD parser never looked at the sample sizes.

Issue #23: a Philips TV on an i5-10310U that declares LPCM of 16 and 20
bits only stayed silent with S32, while the controller logged
"stream 7 dma error: 0x28" (FIFO errors) for as long as the sink was
open; speaker-test sounded with S16_LE and not with S32_LE. Whether the
TV cannot play the 20 bits that it declares, or the 20-bit stream makes
the DMA underrun, the report does not tell apart; 16 bits played.

So the most bits of LPCM that the display declares are read from its
ELD as the codec reads them (AE::PCM::MaxBitsOfSads(): 16, raised to
20 or 24 by an LPCM descriptor, and only an LPCM one) and kept with the
rest of its audio data through a change of display mode
(DisplayAudio::pcmBits, KeepWhileAway()). For a display that declares
16 or 20 bits at most:
- the engine asks the sink for 16-bit PCM (ApplySettingsToFormat(),
  LimitBitsToDisplay(), which logs the decision once at INFO);
- the ALSA sink does not fall back from that request to more bits
  (InitializeHW());
- the output lists no PCM format of more than 16 bits
  (EnumerateDevice(); the list says why: m_maxPcmBits).

The trade-off: a display that declares 20 bits gets 16 bits, which is
what every display with audio takes. A display that declares 24 bits,
which is nearly every one (all the ELDs of the reports of 1.0 but this
one), and a display whose audio data are not known, are as before.
Passthrough is not affected. It goes with "Limit PCM channels to what
the display declares" (audiooutput.hadespcmlimit, expert, on): off, PCM
goes out in as many bits as the output offers, for a display that takes
more than it says; the help of that setting says so now.

Tests: TestAEPcmChannels, with the descriptors of the ELDs of the
reports field by field (the Philips of #23, the LG C5, an LG UltraGear):
21 pass; five mutants of the change are caught.
